WEBVTT

00:00.257 --> 00:04.329
The report refreshes at one in the afternoon and shows 100%.

00:04.329 --> 00:08.215
That saved result used two completed deliveries, both on time.

00:08.215 --> 00:11.207
But a third parcel arrived late at twelve twenty.

00:11.207 --> 00:13.780
Is the report now showing the current result?

00:13.780 --> 00:14.927
Pause and explain.

00:20.330 --> 00:20.980
No.

00:20.980 --> 00:23.381
The calculation rule has not changed.

00:23.381 --> 00:27.056
The saved input gave two out of two, or 100%.

00:27.056 --> 00:32.000
The current delivery records give two out of three, or 66.7%.

00:32.000 --> 00:34.594
Refreshing the report read the older output; it

00:34.594 --> 00:36.629
did not calculate from the updated deliveries.

00:37.667 --> 00:39.963
Follow the later run, R1230.

00:39.963 --> 00:41.730
It failed with an injected destination

00:41.730 --> 00:44.521
write-permission error before publishing a new output.

00:44.521 --> 00:47.419
This is a deliberate fault in the invented simulation.

00:47.419 --> 00:50.016
The failure helps explain the missing publication.

00:50.016 --> 00:52.327
The report's refresh record supplies separate

00:52.327 --> 00:55.075
evidence of what readers actually saw: the old output.

00:55.917 --> 01:00.584
After the simulated permission problem is removed, a new run uses the same calculation

01:00.584 --> 01:05.833
with updated inputs and publishes 66.7% at ten past one.

01:05.832 --> 01:08.339
No further deliveries arrive during recovery.

01:08.339 --> 01:11.966
Does a successful run alone establish that the report has recovered?

01:11.966 --> 01:13.096
Explain before continuing.

01:18.158 --> 01:19.971
Check the report refresh too.

01:19.971 --> 01:26.416
F1312 refreshed at twelve minutes past one and read summary-R1310, the new output

01:26.416 --> 01:28.148
published at ten past one.

01:28.148 --> 01:32.556
Matching that reference shows the new 66.7% reached the report.

01:32.556 --> 01:35.365
We need both the publication and the refresh that read it.

01:36.167 --> 01:39.792
Recovery is one question; correct logic is another.

01:39.792 --> 01:43.121
A successful run can still use the wrong denominator.

01:43.121 --> 01:47.400
The page's separate hypothetical debugging example shows that problem.

01:47.400 --> 01:50.144
Inspect the producing execution, retained code

01:50.144 --> 01:52.599
and input before deciding why a number is wrong.

01:53.596 --> 01:56.274
Keep the earlier output and refresh record.

01:56.274 --> 02:01.111
They explain the 100% readers saw before recovery; that earlier calculation was

02:01.111 --> 02:02.894
correct for its saved inputs.

02:02.894 --> 02:06.788
To reproduce it, retain the input data and code too.

02:06.788 --> 02:09.365
References cannot supply rows that have been deleted.

02:10.250 --> 02:12.087
Try the page's map exercise.

02:12.087 --> 02:15.707
Follow the old Report refresh to Published output and Run.

02:15.707 --> 02:19.212
These map examples show the earlier evidence, not recovery.

02:19.212 --> 02:21.770
Compare them with the recovery table on the page.

02:21.770 --> 02:26.448
Identify the new publication and the refresh that read it, then use Check your answer.
